  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nStarting off with valley fog and comfortable temperatures early this \nmorning. High pressure will be in place today through Saturday \nkeeping conditions generally dry. There is a very low (< 10%) chance \nfor a late day shower or thunderstorm to pop up over the Catskills \nor Central NY where the slow departing trough will still hold some \ninfluence. Otherwise temperatures warm into the upper 70s to mid-\n80s, with generally comfortable humidity levels. Similar conditions \nare expected Saturday with mostly dry conditions through the day. \nSome model guidance (CAMs) are hinting at a surface convergence zone \nwhich could initiate a stray pop up shower or storm in the early \nevening hours near the Catskills once again.\n\nAs an upper level ridge moves in on Saturday, southwesterly flow \nsettles into the region. As a result, temperatures trend slightly \nwarmer with highs in the 80s over the region. Dry conditions will be \nshort lived as another frontal system approaches the region during \nthe overnight hours increasing precipitation chances west of I-81 \nbefore daybreak early Sunday morning. It will be milder and more \nhumid Saturday night, with lows in the 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nBy Sunday, the next deep trough, front and low pressure center will \nbe moving into the area from the Ohio Valley. With southwesterly \nflow out ahead of it, abundant moisture gets advected into the \nregion increasing PWATs between 1.8 to 2.0 inches. It appears a \nseries of warm fronts will lift through the area on Sunday as the \nslow moving low lifts north in southern Ontario Sunday morning. \nModel guidance is trending a bit slower with the eastward \nprogression of this system on Sunday, which could make for a slower \narrival of steady rain and thunderstorms. With that said, it still \nlooks like numerous to widespread showers and scattered \nthunderstorms arrive Sunday afternoon; especially along and west of \nthe I-81 corridor. Most model guidance is keep instability and CAPE \nlevels at a minimum across Central NY and Northeast PA on Sunday. \nHowever, if more daytime heating were to occur, it would be possible \nfor more organized convection to form. Wind shear parameters are \nfavorable, with 0-6 km bulk shear of 40 kts and even low level 0-1km \nshear up to 30 kts indicated on NAM/GFS forecast soundings Sunday \nafternoon.\n\nIt is too early for specific details on exactly how much instability \nwill be present, as that will certainly depend on daytime surface \nheating. The main concern is the risk for excessive heavy rainfall \nas this system moves through. Model guidance does show some timing \ndifferences in when the system will exit. The 12z, 18z and latest \n00z GFS keeps a piece of the upper level low rotating over the Ohio \nValley region through Wednesday, while the ECMWF and Canadian show \nit moving east sometime Monday with an upper level ridge building in \nbehind. The GFS solution would bring the potential for a south to \nnorth oriented heavy rain band to the area during the day on Monday; \nwhen it is also indicating PWATs between 2-2.25 inches near or over \nthe area, along with dew points in the 70s. The moisture advection \nis enhanced further on the GFS solution by mid level convergence and \nstrong low to mid level jet along the western periphery for an \nAtlantic upper level ridge. If this slower eastward progression \ntrend continues, then Monday will certainly need to be watched for \nheavy rain potential as well.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nBeyond this, expect a warming trend for the middle of next week with \nchances for afternoon and evening thunderstorms also in the forecast \neach day. 1000-500mb thicknesses rise between 570-573dm and remain \nsteady at this level through Friday. 850mb temperatures hover \nbetween +14 to +18C in this Tuesday through Friday timeframe. Model \nconsensus from the NBM showers chances for pop up showers and storms \ndue to robust instability levels, plenty of low level moisture (Tds \n65 to 72F)...with the highest PoPs being on Thursday as a shortwave \ntrough approaches from the Great Lakes region. Ensemble consensus is \nfor high temperatures in the low to mid-80s Tuesday, mid to upper \n80s Wednesday and Thursday, before a slight drop back into the low \nto mid-80s Friday. After dew points in the 60s Tuesday afternoon, it \nbecomes more humid and dew points reach around 70s Wednesday and \nThursday next week. If there ends up being a good deal of sunshine, \nheat indices may reach the lower 90s at times mid week.",
